  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nScattered showers and thunderstorms today, some could be\nstrong to possibly severe. Stronger showers and storms may \nalso produce locally heavy rainfall, with the potential for \nlocalized flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA northern stream shortwave trough moves across southern Quebec\ntoday, with a surface cold front moving out ahead of it across\nMaine this afternoon and early this evening. \n\nThere should be sufficient sources of lift from the shortwave\ntrough and the cold front to produce scattered to numerous\nshowers and thunderstorms across the region, starting around\nmidday along the western border with Quebec and ending around\n6-7 pm along the eastern border with New Brunswick.\n\nThe strongest storms appear more likely along and north of a\nGNR-MLT line, where CAPE this afternoon should range from\n1500-2500 J/kg, south of that line CAPE should range from\n500-1500 J/kg and close to 0 near the immediate coast due to the\nmarine layer. The bulk shear is more uniform, with values of\n25-35 kt. There is also progged around 1000-1400 J/kg of\nDowndraft CAPE. The latter, coupled with the freezing level \naround 11-12K across the North and 12-14K further S, and with \nfairly uni- directional winds, yields the main threat being \nfrom strong to locally damaging winds. The storm mode should be \nbowing clusters, with a few discrete cells mainly ahead of those\nclusters. With Corfridi-vectors showing storm motion of \n15-20KT, even with Precipitable Waters of 1.5-1.75\", absent \ntraining, the flood threat is reduced from yesterday. The \npotential for locally heavy rainfall though remains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered rain showers and thunderstorms Monday\nthrough Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nRounds of shortwaves move to the north this week, bringing a \nchance for rain showers and some thunderstorms. Thunder possible\nin the north on Monday, area wide Monday night, and in the \nnorth on Tuesday. More showers are possible late week. Looking \nat potential for 500-1000 J/kg of surface CAPE, 30-40 kt of\nshear, but mid-level lapse rates are weak. A Cold front \nassociated with a shortwave moving through Monday night seems \nto be weak as well. Overall, the environment does not look too \nimpressive for strong thunderstorms, so could just be some brief\nisolated storms below severe criteria. PWATs generally in the\n1.25-1.5 range, so locally heavy rainfall is not expected.",
